LEONARDO ANGELINI received the Ph.D. degree in computer science conducted in a cooperation between the University of Applied Sciences of Western Switzerland (Fribourg) and the University of Fribourg. He is currently a Professor at the School of Management, Fribourg. He also works as a Researcher at the HumanTech Institute. His research interests include human–computer interaction, with particular focus in gesture interaction, tangible interaction, multimodal interaction, affective computing, with several year of experience in vehicular applications and participative design with seniors.

OMAR ABOU KHALED received the B.Sc. degree in computer engineering in 1991, and the master’s and Ph.D. degrees in computer science from the HEUDIASYC Laboratory, Perception and Automatic Control Group, Université de Technologie de Compiègne. He is currently a Professor with the University of Applied Sciences of Western Switzerland. Since 1996, he has been working as a Research Assistant with the MEDIA Group, Theoretical Computer Science Laboratory, EPFL, in the field of educational technologies and web-based training research field on MEDIT and CLASSROOM 2000 projects.

STEFANO CARRINO received the B.Sc. and M.Sc. degrees in electronic engineering from the University of Florence, Italy, and the Ph.D. degree in computer science realized in a cooperation between the University of Applied Sciences of Western Switzerland, Fribourg, and the University of Fribourg. He is currently a Professor of computer science at the University of Applied Sciences of Western Switzerland in Neuchâtel, (HE-Arc). He has been a member of the Data Analytics Research Group, since 2016. He is also responsible or involved in multiple national and international projects in which data analysis, machine learning, and AI solutions play a crucial role. His main research interest includes the conception of machine learning techniques for their application in several domains from Industry 4.0 to advanced human–computer interaction.
ANDREAS SONDEREGGER is a Professor at the Bern University of Applied Sciences in Business and a Lecturer at the University of Fribourg, where he leads the Human Factors Laboratory. He researches and teaches in the fields of cognitive ergonomics, human–computer interaction, and work and organizational psychology.

ELENA MUGELLINI received the master’s degree in telecommunication engineering and the Ph.D. degree in telematics and information society from the University of Florence, in 2002 and 2006, respectively. She is currently a Professor with the Information and Communication Department, University of Applied Sciences of Western Switzerland (Fribourg). She is also a Leader of the HumanTech Institute. She is also a member of the Telematics Technology Laboratory, University of Florence. Her current research interests include ambient intelligent, multimodal interaction, natural interface, intelligent data analysis, and information and knowledge management.

